{# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   PVE ZFS I/O-stall detector (index=os).

   Catches a ZFS backing device that stops completing I/O under a PVE node's
   rpool: the kernel's hung-task watchdog logs a `txg_sync` (ZFS transaction-
   group sync thread) stuck in uninterruptible sleep before the node hard-
   resets. Fires even though the host then crashes, because the line is
   already shipped via pve_syslog_forwarder before the reset. Backtested
   against a real 5-crash incident: matched all 3 crashes that logged before
   dying (verbatim `task:txg_sync ... state:D`), and confirmed a
   systemd-pstore panic-dump line reaches index=os too, though the dump
   content itself does not (closed separately, ansible-proxmox
   crash_diagnostics role: Storage=journal drop-in).
   roles/zfs_fault_alert (ansible-proxmox) only watches zed's own
   checksum/delay event classes, which never fire when the device stops
   completing I/O entirely -- this detector closes that gap.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
[pve_zfs_io_stall]
description = Fires when a PVE node logs a kernel hung-task warning for ZFS's txg_sync thread (state:D) -- an rpool/pool backing device that has stopped completing I/O, usually just before the node hard-resets. Complements roles/zfs_fault_alert (ansible-proxmox), which only sees zed's checksum/delay events and cannot fire when the device stops responding entirely.
search = index=os "state:D" ("txg_sync" OR "blocked for more than")
dispatch.earliest_time = -10m
dispatch.latest_time = now
cron_schedule = */5 * * * *
enableSched = 1
is_visible = 1
disabled = 0
alert.severity = 5
alert.suppress = 1
alert.suppress.period = 30m
alert.suppress.fields = host
alert.track = 1
counttype = number of events
relation = greater than
quantity = 0
